          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance links reflux to meals but does not fully explain the mechanism. The GERD summary (source: \"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)\", general clinical reference) says heartburn and regurgitation are \"often worse after meals, on bending forward, and when lying down,\" and that the pattern relative to meals and position is more informative than severity.\n\nThe same summary lists factors that contribute to reflux, including large or late meals, excess body weight (especially abdominal), hiatus hernia, smoking, alcohol, and pregnancy. It also notes that some medications can worsen it. Large or late meals appear on that list, which may explain why symptoms often follow eating.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not describe the process in detail, such as how the stomach's contents move back into the food pipe after a meal. For that, a doctor or pharmacist is the right person to ask. If reflux symptoms are happening often, or if you have trouble swallowing, weight loss, or chest pain, mention them to a clinician, since these can have other causes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
